WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:07.009
Taron Egerton, Colin Firth and Mark Strong
team up with the Statesman in the superspy

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:08.410
sequel Kingsman: The Golden Circle.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:12.809
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm going to reveal some behind-the-scenes

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:16.379
facts, easter eggs and on-set secrets about
the second Kingsman movie.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.550
I'm also kicking off a new giveaway.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.880
So, for a chance to win this fantastic Kingsman
merchandise, make sure you're subscribed to

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.419
my channel and leave a comment about the movie
below.

00:00:26.419 --> 00:00:30.570
To bring to life the villainous character
of Poppy Adams, who's head of the Golden Circle

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.160
criminal organisation, actress Julianne Moore
and director Matthew Vaughn took inspiration

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.730
from some intriguing places, including the
1978 Superman movie.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:44.120
Julianne Moore especially loved the way Gene
Hackman's Lex Luthor and Valerie Perrine's

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.730
Miss Teschmacher spoke to each other like
regular people despite all their supervillainy,

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.840
and decided to channel that feeling for her
performance as Poppy.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.170
As for the design of Poppyland, which is Poppy's
Americana-inspired lair set in an ancient

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:01.690
ruin in Southeast Asia, Matthew Vaughn went
for a full-on Martha Stewart vibe mixed with

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:07.499
Vegas and Disneyland, because, as Vaughn says,
Poppy is "America's sweetheart gone very wrong".

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:11.850
Speaking of Poppyland, when actor Keith Allen,
who plays one of Poppy's minions in the movie,

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.210
found out that the set for Poppy's diner was
going to be thrown away once filming was finished,

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.770
he decided to rescue it.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:22.030
So, with the producers' permission, Allen
had the set loaded on to a truck and transported

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:26.060
to Stroud in Gloucestershire, where much of
it now features in an American-style diner

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.429
run by his wife.

00:01:28.429 --> 00:01:32.940
Elton John certainly has some memorable scenes
in The Golden Circle; however, he actually

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.700
turned down a part in the first Kingsman movie
that would have shown him throwing tantrums

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.579
and getting in a big fight.

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:43.639
In fact, the original script for Kingsman:
The Secret Service included a whole series

00:01:43.639 --> 00:01:47.600
of cameos by celebrities who were going to
show up when they were abducted by Samuel

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.899
L Jackson's villain Valentine.

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:53.700
But the idea of celebrities actually appearing
in the film as themselves was scrapped as

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.789
director Matthew Vaughn was turned down by
the celebrities he approached!

00:01:56.789 --> 00:02:01.920
By the way, look out for various Elton John
easter eggs during the movie, including nods

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.499
to his songs and albums on the marquee at
Poppy's theatre, and Poppy's robo-dogs are

00:02:06.499 --> 00:02:10.630
even named Bennie and Jet, after John's hit
song Bennie and the Jets.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:16.200
Plus, Elton John's cameo in the sequel also
includes an innuendo-laden call-back to a

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.159
controversial joke from the first Kingsman
movie.

00:02:18.159 --> 00:02:23.590
On the subject of that divisive gag at the
end of The Secret Service, Matthew Vaughn

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.310
and co-screenwriter Jane Goldman have included
an equally contentious moment in the sequel,

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.870
which shows Eggsy planting a tracking device
on a character named Clara played by Poppy

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:36.480
Delevingne, sister of Valerian star Cara Delevingne.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.760
The scene features an intimate moment that
Taron Egerton told Screen Rant he was not

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.310
comfortable with, so instead of the hand which
appears in that shot being Egerton's, it's

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:49.379
actually the hand of Poppy Delevingne's real-life
husband, James Cook.

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:53.340
Apparently, Matthew Vaughn makes a particular
effort to include such controversy-courting

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.800
moments in his movies.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.030
Speaking to Total Film, he stated that, 'It
was [Stanley] Kubrick who said you need 5

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.260
water-cooler moments, and then you've got
a hit film.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.329
So I always try to put 8 in to make sure that
at least 5 happen!'

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:10.030
"For example, the finger scene, in this one,
I know it's gonna ... People are going to

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.760
talk about it ... and ... there'll be a divided
opinion, but there'll be an opinion."

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.780
By the way, I mentioned earlier the controversial
ending of the first Kingsman film, well, Vaughn

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.610
has confessed to Deadline.com that he made
the first cut of that scene a lot more graphic

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:29.270
on purpose as he was deliberately trying to
wind up Emma Watts, 20th-Century Fox's President

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:35.069
of Production, although he also admitted he
completely failed in his efforts to do this.

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:39.689
Spending days tied to a mechanical bull to
film a scene as secret agent Whiskey was not

00:03:39.689 --> 00:03:44.680
actor Pedro Pascal's idea of a good time,
especially as he's had tons of back problems

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.280
since playing Oberyn Martell in Game of Thrones.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:53.459
But after four days in the saddle, at least
the scene was in the can, or so Pascal thought,

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:57.870
that is, until he got a phone call from Matthew
Vaughn, months after the movie wrapped, dropping

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.690
the bombshell that he'd cut the entire mechanical
bull scene from the movie!

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:07.170
While Colin Firth's Kingsman character Harry
Hart was inspired by British actor David Niven

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:12.209
and Michael Caine's fictional spy Harry Palmer
in movies such as The Ipcress Files, Pedro

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:16.380
Pascal's character Agent Whiskey, who's part
of the Statesman American intelligence agency,

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.180
is based on a mix of the Marlboro Man and
actors James Coburn and Burt Reynolds.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:26.080
And to bring the Statesman leader Champagne,
aka Champ, to life, Jeff Bridges drew on memories

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.440
of his actor-father Lloyd Bridges as well
as his previous cowboy roles including Wild

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.009
Bill Hickok.

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:35.800
When it came to the character of Eggsy though,
it was Luke Skywalker's journey over the course

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.840
of the original Star Wars trilogy that really
struck a chord with Matthew Vaughn and he

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.169
kept that in mind when he was outlining Eggsy's
journey in the Golden Circle.

00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:48.551
And as The Golden Circle is the first sequel
Vaughn has directed, he revisited some of

00:04:48.551 --> 00:04:52.790
his favourite cinematic sequels such as The
Godfather: Part II, Aliens, and The Empire

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.660
Strikes Back for inspiration.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.900
If you think that punch in the face Charlie
gives Eggsy during their brawl looked pretty

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.020
painful, that's because it was!

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:07.780
Ed Holcroft, who plays rejected-Kingsman-applicant-turned-villain
Charlie, misjudged the angle and ended up

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:12.220
smashing his friend Kingsman star Taron Egerton
straight in the face, with the full force

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.150
of his thump landing right on Egerton's nose.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:18.930
Obviously, Egerton was pretty annoyed at the
time, but the filmmakers thought it looked

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.550
so good they decided to use that take in the
final movie!

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:27.889
And that wasn't Egerton's only on-set injury
while filming the Kingsman sequel.

00:05:27.889 --> 00:05:31.520
During a break between takes, he was sat on
top of a taxi they were using for a stunt

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.400
sequence when a member of the crew walked
past and slammed the cab door shut, not realising

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.980
that Egerton's hand was in the way!

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:40.080
Ouch!

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.020
The action scene featuring that taxi cab includes
a nod to the 1977 movie The Spy Who Loved

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.979
Me which stars Roger Moore as James Bond.

00:05:47.979 --> 00:05:52.570
In that film, Bond escapes the baddies by
putting his Lotus Esprit car into submersible

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.380
mode and going underwater.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:58.520
Kingsman director Matthew Vaughn loved that
scene and wanted to recreate the moment but

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:03.870
in a much more attainable car for people watching
in the audience, hence the taxi cab.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:08.629
And talking of Bond, did you notice the 'bottled
in Bond' mark on the Statesman liquor that

00:06:08.629 --> 00:06:10.680
Merlin and Eggsy find in the vault?

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.990
'Bottled in Bond' is a term used in the American
drinks industry that means the whisky has

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:19.741
been made to a certain standard, but coincidentally
it's also a nice wink to the fact that the

00:06:19.741 --> 00:06:23.210
Kingsman movies owe a great debt to the Bond
films.

